I don’t disagree that “reducing inflation works if it’s tried”. I do disagree that trying it singularly with monetary policy while fiscal policy rows in the other direction is a fool’s errand. Maybe hiking 2 times of 25bps each will do wonders. I somehow rather doubt it. You’d need a lot more if the goal is to reach 2% quickly. I don’t see where you’d get that type of realistic political capital for the Fed. So maybe doing something is better than doing nothing except doing something makes the other factors feel like the Fed has got it under control while they could go on doing whatever they were doing.

@EconBerger @SteveMatthews12 @AdamPosen What’s the basis of your assessment that it wouldn’t take much or any tightening for inflation to return on track to 2%? If so what’s the point of the debate?

No, the reason is that the inflationary shock is a combination of supply shocks and business investment demand shock and little of consumer demand shock. Consumers are fine but not the driver of overheating. Supply shocks don’t respond to modest tightening. Neither does AI capex likely unless the Fed has the credibility to commit to long run tight policy which requires forward guidance that was just forsaken. 🤷🏻‍♂️

